<http://data.silknow.org/object/a41b7551-b4a8-3ae3-a13e-8f658fde100d>
        a                              ecrm:E22_Man-Made_Object ;
        rdfs:comment                   "Gota, or ribbon woven of silk and strips of silver, was made in varying widths to trim garments. Used all over South Asia, it could be plain silver or gilded silver, patterned with coloured silks, or embossed like this example. Before cheaper copper replaced silver in the 20th century, old garments with heavy gota trimming were often burned to melt down and extract the valuable metal."@en , "Length of gota (ribbon), woven silk and metal strip, embossed, Delhi, c.1855; Textiles"@en , "Wide length of silk and silver-strip woven ribbon (gota). Embossed with a large repeating diamond pattern, each diamond containing an alternating central patten; grided blossoms, diagonal floral scrolls, and a large stem of flowering poppies. The central diamond in the length contains a sun-like circle design. One end cut, the other finished by a band of red silk and raw fringe."@en , "GOTA (RIBBON)\nWoven silk and silver strips, embossed\nDelhi\nc. 1855\n\nGota, or ribbon woven of silk and strips of silver, was made in varying widths to trim garments. Used all over South Asia, it could be plain silver or gilded silver, patterned with coloured silks, or embossed like this example. Before cheaper copper replaced silver in the 20th century, old garments with heavy gota trimming were often burned to melt down and extract the valuable metal. [01/08/2017]"@en ;
        ecrm:P138i_has_representation  <http://data.silknow.org/image/ab51d808-a0a0-34e1-adf4-386eb547b66d> ;
        ecrm:P3_has_note               "GOTA (RIBBON)\nWoven silk and silver strips, embossed\nDelhi\nc. 1855\n\nGota, or ribbon woven of silk and strips of silver, was made in varying widths to trim garments. Used all over South Asia, it could be plain silver or gilded silver, patterned with coloured silks, or embossed like this example. Before cheaper copper replaced silver in the 20th century, old garments with heavy gota trimming were often burned to melt down and extract the valuable metal. [01/08/2017]"@en , "Wide length of silk and silver-strip woven ribbon (gota). Embossed with a large repeating diamond pattern, each diamond containing an alternating central patten; grided blossoms, diagonal floral scrolls, and a large stem of flowering poppies. The central diamond in the length contains a sun-like circle design. One end cut, the other finished by a band of red silk and raw fringe."@en , "Gota, or ribbon woven of silk and strips of silver, was made in varying widths to trim garments. Used all over South Asia, it could be plain silver or gilded silver, patterned with coloured silks, or embossed like this example. Before cheaper copper replaced silver in the 20th century, old garments with heavy gota trimming were often burned to melt down and extract the valuable metal."@en , "Length of gota (ribbon), woven silk and metal strip, embossed, Delhi, c.1855; Textiles"@en ;
